      "When residents of Bellford complained that official weather reports rarely matched conditions in their neighbourhoods, the local council initially treated the comments as ordinary conversation. The nearest professional station was only twenty kilometres away, so its forecasts appeared close enough. Yet Bellford lies between a steep ridge and a wide estuary. Cold air collects in some streets, while strong coastal winds affect others. A single regional figure could not describe these differences accurately.",
      "A group of teachers, gardeners and engineering students proposed a network of low-cost sensors. The devices would record temperature, rainfall and wind speed every ten minutes, then send the information to a public website. Before the project began, the team tested each sensor beside professional equipment. This revealed that several inexpensive models produced unreliable readings in direct sunlight, so protective covers were added and the data was checked regularly.",
      "The network soon became useful in ways its organisers had not expected. Gardeners used it to protect young plants from sudden frost, while schools compared conditions in different parts of town. During one severe storm, emergency workers identified streets where water levels were rising fastest. However, the sensors did not replace national forecasts. They added local detail to wider observations and helped residents understand why weather can vary over a short distance.",
